Baker Street persona pack — sources
===================================

The `watson` persona is an original prose description of a narrative voice, written
for this pack. It is a *characterization* — diction, temperament, descriptive habits —
and contains no text from any source work.

It was synthesized by reading two stories by Arthur Conan Doyle, both long in the
public domain in the United States (published 1887 and 1892, well before the 1929
cutoff):

  - "A Study in Scarlet" (1887)
      Project Gutenberg eBook #244 — https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/244
      Read for: Watson's self-introduction (the Afghan campaign, Maiwand, the
      invalided army surgeon), and his cataloguing habit — the pencilled list of
      a friend's accomplishments and limits.

  - "The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes" (1892)
      Project Gutenberg eBook #1661 — https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1661
      Read for: "A Scandal in Bohemia" (the retrospective frame, the precisely
      dated evening, the admiring aside) and "The Adventure of the Speckled Band"
      (the chronicler's opening over his notes, the clinical-then-human read of a
      frightened client, discretion named rather than performed).

The underlying stories are public domain; Project Gutenberg's trademark applies to
its own branding and editions, none of which is reproduced here. Only the free texts
were consulted, and no passage of them appears in this pack.

The character of Dr. John H. Watson as depicted in these early works is likewise in
the public domain in the United States.
